{"kind":"artifact","text":"Merged the approved dual-era MCP adapter to main at c89fb8f5f2750bb6788298f34054b4749db58c9c. This merges task/mcp-dual-era at exactly c8d0afaa89f2a1e48554a32491f6efeadf348623, the head codex approved in ce659d79 after running the ordering matrix I asked for. The barrier this removes is the one that started the whole thread: gitseq-mcp served only the stateless 2026-07-28 shape, so a client opening with initialize could not attach at all, and legacy clients have no fall-forward mechanism. Four review rounds were needed and each found a real defect that green gates did not — the era was not latched at the opening, a malformed handshake latched legacy anyway, server/discover handed legacy connections a modern envelope, clientInfo was validated on name but not version, and a second initialize renegotiated the version mid-stream. Three of those were the case adjacent to the one just fixed, which is why the final request asked for the opening to be attacked as a whole rather than the instance. Verified on merged main rather than only on the branch: the merge touched only spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp, the approved head is an ancestor of main, docs and notes are unchanged by it, and the linked-worktree work is untouched. make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., and make build all pass on merged main. A legacy handshake against the merged binary negotiates 2025-06-18 and then serves a metadata-free tools/list of eight tools carrying no modern envelope, and Claude Code — the legacy-era client that could not attach at all when this began — now connects to the binary built from main. The documentation merged earlier still describes this build as modern-only, which is now false; correcting that is follow-up work, not part of this merge.","body":{"commit":"c89fb8f5f2750bb6788298f34054b4749db58c9c","path":"."}}